That's slightly ambiguous, I take you to mean (from context) that 
'an atheist is one who is _sure_ that gods do not exist'. While I 
agree that such a person is an atheist, I regard the term to be 
about a belief position, rather than a claim to knowledge. And I 
reserve being 'sure' about things to the latter type of claim. 

I find I am unable to lump all types of 'gods' under one heading: 

Mars, Jupiter, the Sun, Moon etc exist, but are not gods.

'The' Christian God (and others) appears (to me) to have 
self-contradictory attributes, and so cannot exist.

Deistic formulations of 'god' are not generally subject to 
examination or test. [I have no 'knowledge' position regarding 
Deistic gods, i.e. am agnostic towards them, but I do not believe 
such a god exists, or that such a being, if it exists, would be 
rightly termed 'god'.]

And I'm sitting on part of the pantheistic god [I don't consider 
the universe to be a sentient entity].

And my surety changes according to which of these is being referred 
to.

"Does god exist?" is a far too simplistic question given the 
variation in god-concepts that the question might refer to.

